From: Michael C. Ferguson <michael.christopher.ferguson@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 17:00:00 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] fbdev: MiSTer_fb: mmap the whole region, add FBIO_MISTER_FLUSH

Origin:       original, this repo (not from the fork, not upstream).
Author:       Michael C. Ferguson; applies on top of 0001 (Sorgelig's driver).
Upstream:     No (MiSTer-specific driver). Carried in de10nano and, by symlink,
              the beta (7.x RT) series; 0001's text is left untouched so it keeps
              its parity with stock.
Disposition:  carry -- feature. Lets a userspace client (the greenfield HD OSD)
              write its frames through fb_io_mmap's write-combined mapping
              instead of /dev/mem O_SYNC (strongly ordered): a 1080p RGB565 frame
              takes 38 ms strongly ordered and 4.2 ms write-combined on the
              DE10-Nano (owner's rig, 2026-10-09).

Four changes, all inside drivers/video/fbdev/MiSTer_fb.c:

1. fix.smem_len = resource_size - 4096 (the whole DT region past the header
   page), so mmap() reaches every frame a client places in the region, not
   just the first one. yres_virtual stays == yres, so fbcon and fbset see the
   same geometry as before. screen_size is set to one visible frame, so
   read(2)/write(2) on /dev/fb0 stay bounded exactly as smem_len bounded them
   (fb_sys_read/fb_sys_write use screen_size when it is non-zero).

2. FBIO_MISTER_FLUSH = _IO('F', 0xA0) = 0x000046A0, no argument, returns 0:
   wmb(). It runs under the fb_info lock the fbdev core takes around every
   driver ioctl, which change 4 keeps short. On this SoC (CONFIG_ARM_HEAVY_MB,
   CONFIG_OUTER_CACHE_SYNC, PL310) wmb() is dsb(st) followed by
   outer_cache.sync(), which drains the PL310 store buffer. The FPGA scaler
   reads DDR through an f2sdram port that does not pass the L2, so a client
   that wrote a frame through the write-combined mapping calls this before it
   hands the frame to the scaler. Userspace can issue "dsb st" but cannot sync
   the PL310. 'F' 0xA0 is free: ioctl-number.rst lists 'F' driver-private
   numbers 00-0F, 20, 80-8F and DD only, and no header in include/ uses 'F'
   0xA0-0xDC. Unpatched kernels answer -ENOTTY, which is how userspace probes
   for it.

3. Writing the `mode` parameter clears the header page and the new mode's
   visible frame (screen_size bytes), not the whole region. Stock memsets the
   full resource, which would wipe a second frame a client keeps further up
   the region and, if the DT region ever grows, bytes other users own (SNES's
   MSU track resumes at 0x22800000). Nothing outside the visible frame was
   ever displayed, so what the screen shows after a mode write is unchanged.

4. FBIO_WAITFORVSYNC drops info->lock for its wait (unlock_fb_info; wait;
   lock_fb_info). fbdev core (fb_chrdev.c, 6.18 and 7.x) calls the driver's
   fb_ioctl under lock_fb_info, so a client looping on the vsync wait (the
   greenfield T-FRAME seat, SCHED_FIFO) held the lock for up to a frame, or
   VSYNC_TIMEOUT_MSEC with no vsync, and every other ioctl on the fb queued
   behind it: FBIO_MISTER_FLUSH slept a frame or two, and on PREEMPT_RT the
   FIFO waiter would re-take the rtmutex ahead of a SCHED_OTHER flusher. The
   wait reads only frame_count and vs_wait, never info; the open file holds a
   reference on info for the ioctl's duration, so it cannot go away while
   unlocked. Stock behaviour for a single waiter (Main_MiSTer) is unchanged.

The kernel's own mapping stays memremap(MEMREMAP_WT), as stock has it. It
cannot fight the user's write-combined alias: on 32-bit ARM ioremap_wt is
ioremap_wc (arch/arm/include/asm/io.h), Normal non-cacheable like the mmap's
pgprot_writecombine (only shareability differs: MT_DEVICE_WC lacks
L_PTE_SHARED, benign because socfpga.dtsi sets arm,shared-override on the
PL310); and where WT is genuinely cached its lines are never dirty, so no
write-back can overwrite a user store. fbcon's own drawing is held off while a
client owns the screen (the greenfield HD OSD keeps the console in
KD_GRAPHICS).

Visible side effect: the probe line now reports the region length, e.g.
"frame buffer device at 0x22001000+0x7ff000" instead of "+0x12c000".

Signed-off-by: Michael C. Ferguson <michael.christopher.ferguson@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/video/fbdev/MiSTer_fb.c | 67 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 62 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/video/fbdev/MiSTer_fb.c
+++ b/drivers/video/fbdev/MiSTer_fb.c
@@ -27,6 +27,22 @@
 #define PALETTE_SIZE         256
 #define DRIVER_NAME          "MiSTer_fb"
 #define VSYNC_TIMEOUT_MSEC   50
+#define HEADER_SIZE          4096
+
+/*
+ * FBIO_MISTER_FLUSH: drain this CPU's buffered stores to DDR, including the
+ * PL310 outer-cache store buffer, before userspace hands a frame it wrote
+ * through the write-combined mmap to the FPGA scaler (which reads DDR over
+ * an f2sdram port, past the L2).  Userspace can issue "dsb st" itself but
+ * cannot sync the PL310; wmb() on ARM is dsb(st) plus outer_sync().  No
+ * argument, returns 0.  It never waits on a vsync: the fbdev core's
+ * info->lock around it is held only by short paths, because
+ * FBIO_WAITFORVSYNC drops it for its wait.  'F' 0xA0 is unused in
+ * Documentation/userspace-api/ioctl/ioctl-number.rst and include/ (the 'F'
+ * driver-private numbers there are 0x00-0x0F, 0x20, 0x80-0x8F and 0xDD).
+ * A kernel without this patch answers -ENOTTY, so userspace can probe it.
+ */
+#define FBIO_MISTER_FLUSH    _IO('F', 0xA0)
 
 static u32 width = 0, height = 0, stride = 0, format = 0, rb = 1, frame_count = 0, res_count = 0;
 module_param(width,  uint, 0444);
@@ -122,7 +138,22 @@
 			break;
 		}
 
+		/*
+		 * The fbdev core calls every driver ioctl under info->lock, and
+		 * this wait lasts up to a frame (VSYNC_TIMEOUT_MSEC with no
+		 * vsync).  Holding the lock across it would queue every other
+		 * ioctl on this fb (FBIO_MISTER_FLUSH, FBIOGET_*) behind the
+		 * waiter.  The wait reads only frame_count and vs_wait, never
+		 * info, and the open file holds a reference on info, so the lock
+		 * is dropped for the wait and retaken for the core's unlock.
+		 */
+		unlock_fb_info(info);
 		ret = fb_wait_for_vsync(ioarg);
+		lock_fb_info(info);
+		break;
+	case FBIO_MISTER_FLUSH:
+		wmb();
+		ret = 0;
 		break;
 	default:
 		ret = -ENOTTY;
@@ -162,6 +193,17 @@
  *
  * The drawing ops stay on the sys_* helpers, as in 5.15: memremap() hands
  * back a normal-memory mapping, so direct dereference is correct there.
+ *
+ * The kernel mapping stays MEMREMAP_WT, as stock has it, while userspace
+ * may write the same bytes through fb_io_mmap's write-combined mapping.
+ * The two aliases cannot fight: on 32-bit ARM ioremap_wt IS ioremap_wc
+ * (arch/arm/include/asm/io.h), Normal non-cacheable like
+ * pgprot_writecombine.  Only shareability differs (MT_DEVICE_WC lacks
+ * L_PTE_SHARED), which is benign because socfpga.dtsi sets
+ * arm,shared-override on the PL310.  Where WT is truly cached its lines are
+ * never dirty, so no write-back can overwrite a userspace store.  The kernel's
+ * own drawing (fbcon) is held off while a client owns the screen: the HD
+ * OSD keeps the console in KD_GRAPHICS.
  */
 static const struct fb_ops ops = {
 	.owner = THIS_MODULE,
@@ -267,7 +309,17 @@
 
 	dev_info(&fbdev->pdev->dev, "width = %u, height = %u, format=%u\n", info->var.xres, info->var.yres, format);
 
-	info->fix.smem_len = info->fix.line_length * info->var.yres;
+	/*
+	 * mmap() covers the whole DT region past the header page, so a client
+	 * can place more than one frame in it (the HD OSD's two slots).  The
+	 * visible geometry is unchanged (yres_virtual == yres, so fbcon and
+	 * fbset behave as before), and read(2)/write(2) stay bounded to one
+	 * visible frame through screen_size, as smem_len bounded them before.
+	 */
+	info->fix.smem_len = resource_size(fbdev->fb_res) - HEADER_SIZE;
+	info->screen_size = min_t(unsigned long,
+				  (unsigned long)info->fix.line_length * info->var.yres,
+				  info->fix.smem_len);
 
 	info->pseudo_palette = fbdev->fb_base;
 
@@ -296,8 +348,8 @@
 	}
 
 	retval = setup_fb_info(fbdev);
-	fbdev->info.fix.smem_start = fbdev->fb_res->start+4096;
-	fbdev->info.screen_base = fbdev->fb_base+4096;
+	fbdev->info.fix.smem_start = fbdev->fb_res->start+HEADER_SIZE;
+	fbdev->info.screen_base = fbdev->fb_base+HEADER_SIZE;
 
 	retval = fb_alloc_cmap(&fbdev->info.cmap, PALETTE_SIZE, 0);
 	if (retval < 0) return retval;
@@ -377,11 +429,16 @@
 		console_lock();
 		lock_fb_info(&p_fbdev->info);
 
-		memset(p_fbdev->fb_base, 0, resource_size(p_fbdev->fb_res));
-
 		sscanf(val, "%u %u %u %u %u", &format, &rb, &width, &height, &stride);
 		setup_fb_info(p_fbdev);
 
+		/*
+		 * Clear the header page and the new mode's visible frame only, not
+		 * the whole region: another client's frames (the HD OSD's second
+		 * slot) or, if the region grows, another user's bytes live past it.
+		 */
+		memset(p_fbdev->fb_base, 0, HEADER_SIZE + p_fbdev->info.screen_size);
+
 		fb_set(&p_fbdev->info);
 		unlock_fb_info(&p_fbdev->info);
 
